**Theorem 22.2 (forced predecessor and block/gap inverse).**
$F_{\rm blk}$ is injective on all of $\mathcal H_{\rm blk}$,
including arbitrary finite unknown gaps and alpha. Its inverse
is the following finite exact procedure on its image.

At a non-alpha $(u,y)$, reverse $T_u$ while every coordinate is
positive. At the first zero let $q$ be the inversion count and
$W=\{a:y_a>0\}$. Parse $W$ as the material support of a term $s$
by the original terminal/unary/binary arity rules. Compare $s$ with
$u$: at each old branch compare the two subtrees recursively; an
old logical leaf must be unchanged or changed by exactly its one
enabled edit. The changed old leaves are the last nonempty block.
Restrict $y$ to $W$ and repeat on $s$. At alpha, double the scalar
until one to recover $q_0$. Reverse the recovered block list,
retaining each recovered following gap.

Proof. Before every block the field on all its current vertices is
positive. Initially it is $2^{-q_0}>0$; after each independent block,
Theorem 21.2 makes one Tick positive everywhere, and any additional
Tick preserves positivity. Positive internal gaps establish the
induction. Immediately after a block its zeros are exactly the union
of its fresh supports. Coordinates always lie in $[0,1]$ and the
field is nonzero by the same positive contraction/lattice induction
as Observation 3.3.

For the actual last block $B:s\to u$ and final gap $q_m$,

$$
x_h=T_u^{q_m}\iota_Bz,\qquad z>0\text{ on }V_s.
\tag{22.2}
$$

If $q_m=0$ there are already fresh zeros. If $q_m>0$, every reversed
cut before the $q_m$th is a positive power $T_u^r\iota_Bz$ with
$r\ge1$, positive everywhere by Theorem 21.2. At the $q_m$th it
is precisely $\iota_Bz$. Thus the first zero forces exactly $q_m$
and the positive support $W=V_s$; no selected collapse or search
among smaller predecessors is needed.

AA Definition 17.3 parses this old support uniquely, including unary
markers. At each old branch, no block event is allowed at that branch
and its root constructor persists. At an old alpha leaf, the only
changed possibility is beta; at an old beta leaf, it is literally
$\langle\beta,\alpha\rangle$. These comparisons with the whole current
$u$ determine which old leaves changed, hence exactly the typed block.
They also distinguish an unchanged unary marker from a new logical
position. Restriction recovers the actual $z$.

Each block removes at least one material vertex in this local reverse
calculation. Applying (22.2) inductively therefore reaches the actual
alpha and its scalar $2^{-q_0}$; doubling recovers $q_0$ uniquely.
This proves decoding after actual evaluation is identity.
Conversely the scalar equation and every stored equation (22.2)
replay the actual legal zero extensions and Ticks. Thus evaluating
the decoded history returns the input pair. Every support, block and
counter was forced, which proves injectivity and both inverse
identities. All reversals are local rational computations; no source
vertex is deleted or physically reversed. $\square$

